In C++, the explicit keyword is a specifier used to prevent the compiler from performing unintended implicit type conversions or copy-initializations
By default, if a class has a constructor that can be called with a single argument, the C++ compiler treats it as a converting constructor. This means the compiler will automatically and silently
convert that single argument's data type into an object of your class whenever it feels it is necessary to make the code compile. Marking a constructor or conversion operator as explicit 
shuts down this behavior, forcing the programmer to write clear, deliberate code


Why is explicit Needed? (The Problem)

Imagine a class that wraps a dynamic array buffer:

#include <iostream>

class Buffer {
public:
    // Converting constructor (takes a single argument)
    Buffer(int size) {
        std::cout << "Created a buffer of size " << size << "\n";
    }
};

void printBuffer(const Buffer& buf) {
    // Expects a Buffer object
}

int main() {
    Buffer b = 10;      // Implicit conversion: Looks like an integer assignment, but calls Buffer(10)
    printBuffer(20);    // Implicit conversion: Accidentally passes an int where a Buffer is expected!
}


Why this is dangerous: Passing 20 to printBuffer is syntactically legal to the compiler. It will silently construct a temporary Buffer of size 20. 
If a developer accidentally types printBuffer(some_index) instead of passing an actual buffer object, the compiler will not catch the typo, hiding subtle runtime bugs


The Solution: Using explicit

Adding the explicit keyword before the constructor requires you to use direct initialization or an explicit type cast

class Buffer {
public:
    // Added explicit keyword
    explicit Buffer(int size) {
        std::cout << "Created a buffer of size " << size << "\n";
    }
};

void printBuffer(const Buffer& buf) {}

int main() {
    // Buffer b1 = 10;   // COMPILER ERROR! Implicit conversion is forbidden.
    // printBuffer(20);  // COMPILER ERROR! Cannot implicitly convert int to Buffer.

    // Correct explicitly defined ways:
    Buffer b2(10);                    // Direct initialization (Allowed)
    Buffer b3 = Buffer(10);           // Explicit creation (Allowed)
    printBuffer(Buffer(20));          // Explicit temporary creation (Allowed)
    printBuffer(static_cast<Buffer>(20)); // Explicit type casting (Allowed)
}



Modern C++ Extensions

Over time, the C++ standard expanded how explicit can be applied:

1. Explicit Conversion Operators (Since C++11)

Just like constructors convert a foreign type → your class, custom conversion operators convert your class → a foreign type. You can make these explicit to avoid accidental evaluation

class Handle {
public:
    // Prevent accidental conversion to bool in math equations
    explicit operator bool() const { return true; }
};

int main() {
    Handle h;
    // bool flag = h;        // ERROR: Implicit conversion disabled
    bool flag = static_cast<bool>(h); // OK

    if (h) { /* ... */ }    // OK: Condition checks still allow implicit evaluation
}



2. Conditional explicit(bool) (Since C++20)

Introduced in C++20, you can make a constructor conditionally explicit using a boolean constant expression. This is widely used in template programming (like std::pair or std::tuple)

template<typename T>
class Wrapper {
public:
    // Explicit ONLY if T is an integral type (like int, char)
    explicit(std::is_integral_v<T>) Wrapper(T val) {}
};


Summary of Best Practices

• Always use explicit on constructors that take a single argument unless you have a specific architectural reason to 
allow automatic conversion (e.g., a custom String class converting a const char*)
Apply explicit to conversion operators to avoid your objects being silently evaluated inside standard arithmetic operations
